Let a kernel with no pthread kext finish bsd_init instead of panicking.

bsd_init() reaches, without even a trace line in front of it:

    bsd/kern/bsd_init.c:788
        pthread_init();

and that is:

    bsd/pthread/pthread_shims.c
        void
        pthread_init(void)
        {
                if (!pthread_functions) {
                        panic("pthread kernel extension not loaded "
                              "(function table is NULL).");
                }
                pthread_functions->pthread_init();
        }

pthread_functions is filled in by pthread_kext_register(), whose only caller
lives in com.apple.kec.pthread -- the kext half of Apple's libpthread project,
which is not part of xnu. A kernel built from this tree alone has no kexts, so
the table stays NULL and the boot dies at that line. It is the same shape of
problem as read_random() needing corecrypto, and it is handled the same way.

Nineteen entry points in that file dereference the table. The table itself is
not worth stubbing: struct pthread_functions_s is about a hundred and ten
function pointers with a compile-time assert on its size, so a stub would be a
hundred no-op functions that still cannot do any of the work, and a mistake in
it would show up as a wild call rather than an error. Guarding the entry points
is smaller, and it fails in a way that names what is missing.

So:

  - pthread_init() prints one line and returns instead of panicking;
  - pth_proc_hashinit() and pth_proc_hashdelete(), which fork and exit call on
    every process, become no-ops;
  - the bsdthread_* and psynch_* syscalls return ENOTSUP, which is already the
    idiom in this file for the ones Apple never implemented (psynch_rw_upgrade,
    psynch_rw_downgrade, psynch_rw_unlock2);
  - kdp_pthread_find_owner() and kdp_pthread_get_thread_kwq() return without
    touching the table, so a panic stackshot cannot fault on it.

In bsdthread_register() and bsdthread_terminate() the guard goes after the
existing declarations rather than at the top of the block, so that no statement
precedes a declaration. Neither function can do anything meaningful before the
guard: the work in both is behind the table.

What this buys is the rest of bsd_init, which is worth having -- the next thing
of substance after this line is vfs_mountroot(). What it does not buy is a
working userland. libpthread's runtime calls bsdthread_register() during the
startup of every process, so the first thing that runs will get ENOTSUP and
nothing threaded will work. That is not a reason to keep the panic: there is no
root filesystem to run anything from yet. When there is one, the kern/ half of
libpthread has to be built into this kernel, and these guards then simply stop
being taken.

--- a/bsd/pthread/pthread_shims.c
+++ b/bsd/pthread/pthread_shims.c
@@ -308,13 +308,23 @@
 	return kr;
 }
 
+/*
+ * This kernel is built without the pthread kernel extension, so nothing ever
+ * calls pthread_kext_register() and pthread_functions stays NULL. Every shim
+ * below dereferences it; see the patch header for why the table is guarded
+ * rather than stubbed out.
+ */
+#define PTHREAD_KEXT_ABSENT()   (pthread_functions == NULL)
+
 /* kernel (core) to kext shims */
 
 void
 pthread_init(void)
 {
-	if (!pthread_functions) {
-		panic("pthread kernel extension not loaded (function table is NULL).");
+	if (PTHREAD_KEXT_ABSENT()) {
+		printf("pthread: kernel extension not loaded; pthread and "
+		    "workqueue syscalls will return ENOTSUP\n");
+		return;
 	}
 	pthread_functions->pthread_init();
 }
@@ -322,12 +332,18 @@
 void
 pth_proc_hashinit(proc_t p)
 {
+	if (PTHREAD_KEXT_ABSENT()) {
+		return;
+	}
 	pthread_functions->pth_proc_hashinit(p);
 }
 
 void
 pth_proc_hashdelete(proc_t p)
 {
+	if (PTHREAD_KEXT_ABSENT()) {
+		return;
+	}
 	pthread_functions->pth_proc_hashdelete(p);
 }
 
